				1148,
				2
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Introduce webview lifecycle state and message envelope types\n\nDefine the `WebviewLifecycleState::Enum` and `WebviewMessage::Struct` within the `Environment/WebviewProvider` module to establish the core data contracts for Tauri webview management.\n\nThe `WebviewLifecycleState::Enum` mirrors the VS Code webview state machine, explicitly defining the lifecycle transitions from `Unloaded` through `Loading`, `Loaded`, `Visible`, `Hidden`, to `Disposed`. The `WebviewMessage::Struct` serves as the serialization envelope for host-to-webview communication, encapsulating the `MessageIdentifier`, `MessageType` tag, `Payload`, and optional `Source` context.\n\nBoth types adhere to the project's strict PascalCase naming conventions and are configured with `Serialize` and `Deserialize` traits to support JSON transmission over the Tauri event bus, laying the groundwork for the `Wind` frontend service layer to manage native webview panel lifecycles.",
			"SHA": "c10618c8025d32eac34106831406614f1f7eb38a"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-02T09:05:33Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1146,
				2
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Introduce atomic performance and throughput metrics modules\n\nEstablish the `PerformanceMetrics` and `ThroughputMetrics` structs within `Source/IPC/Common` to provide a centralized mechanism for tracking IPC performance across the Mountain backend. Adhering to the strict PascalCase naming convention (`Struct::Struct`, `Fn::Fn`), these modules define state containers for latency (average/peak), throughput (messages/bytes per second), resource utilization (CPU/memory), and error rates.\n\nThe `PerformanceMetrics::Struct` implements an unbiased running mean for latency updates via `RecordMessage::Fn` and includes logic for calculating success rates and latency threshold violations. The `ThroughputMetrics::Struct` captures per-direction counters with a fixed start time to derive rates dynamically. These new types serve as the foundation for the `Echo` scheduler and `Mist` communication layers, enabling precise observability of the gRPC and Tauri IPC boundaries without relying on external telemetry agents.",
			"SHA": "2be1a2667b6af7ed494d04fbe7f5a14bfb3cbd86"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-02T09:05:21Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1143,
				5
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Atomize scheduler initialization into dedicated PascalCase modules\n\nDecompose the runtime scheduler bring-up logic into a focused directory of modules (`Build`, `BuildDebug`, `BuildWithConfig`, `CreateBuilder`, `SchedulerConfig`) to enforce the project's `Struct::Struct` and `Fn` naming conventions. The new `SchedulerConfig::Struct` defines tuning knobs for worker count, metrics, and log levels that adapt automatically to the active build profile (`Debug`, `Telemetry`, `Development`).\n\n`Build::Fn` and `BuildDebug::Fn` serve as profile-specific entry points that delegate to `BuildWithConfig::Fn`, which orchestrates the creation of an `Echo` scheduler instance via `CreateBuilder::Fn`. The builder logic clamps worker counts to the `[1, 256]` range to prevent misconfiguration. This change isolates the scheduler boot path from the binary initialization, providing atomic, testable units for the `Echo` integration while maintaining strict separation of concerns between configuration, construction, and execution.",
			"SHA": "7469788102dc319e5db53b52b342a961b70d2401"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
